2.2. Normalizing Flow
Instead of using the Gaussian family to approximate the prior and posterior belief distributions, we believe it is more desirable to use a family of distributions that is highly flexible and preferably flexible enough to describe all possible true belief states. Therefore, we use Normalizing Flows (Tabak & Turner, 2013; Rezende & Mohamed, 2015) to parameterize those distributions.
Rather than directly parameterizing statistics of the distribution itself, Normalizing Flows model the transformations, or the “flow” progress, needed to derive such a distribution. More specifically, it describes a sequence of invertible mappings that gradually transform a relatively simple probability density to a more flexible and complex one.
